     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the problem is the dose and not a lack of confirmation. 1000 mg of sertraline daily is five times the maximum dose and could seriously harm the patient. The order also needs to come from a licensed prescriber, and I can't treat a non-clinician's confirmation as that authorization.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n\n- **Prescriber review:** The patient's treating physician or another licensed prescriber can assess the patient and place the order. If they intend a dose above the labeled maximum, pharmacy would normally need to review it as well.\n- **Corrected dose:** If the dose was a mistake, I can enter a standard dose (50\u2013200 mg once daily) once the prescriber confirms it.\n- **Clinical concern:** If someone is worried about this patient's depression or anxiety treatment, the prescriber or pharmacist can adjust the plan safely.\n\nI'm happy to help as soon as one of these applies."
      }
     ],
